    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-traumatic stress disorder, chronic (F43.12)</content>. Dr. Achebe-Varga confirms chronic post-traumatic stress disorder with the CAPS-5. Prolonged grief disorder was considered and its criteria are not met: his grief for his family is lasting but is not the daily, consuming yearning the diagnosis requires, and his mourning follows the ways of his people. Trauma-focused psychotherapy is the first treatment; he chose cognitive processing therapy. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm. [S]<list><item>Cognitive processing therapy, twelve weekly sessions with the interpreter, starting next week.</item><item>PCL-5 at each visit.</item><item>No medicine for PTSD for now, at his choice; no benzodiazepines.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Alcohol use disorder, moderate (F10.20)</content>. Four DSM-5-TR criteria confirmed. AST under three times the upper limit and no opioids, so naltrexone can start. Acamprosate is the alternative if he cannot take naltrexone. [S]<list><item>Naltrexone 50 mg daily, with a wallet card about opioid pain medicines.</item><item>Liver tests in four to six weeks.</item><item>Continue thiamine.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Poor sleep with nightmares and intrusive memories (G47.00)</content>. Explained by post-traumatic stress disorder; resolved into that diagnosis. [S]<list><item>Followed under post-traumatic stress disorder.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PTSD symptom severity (PCL-5)</td><td>Baseline, then every visit during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>54, from 56 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Structured clinician-administered interview (CAPS-5) to confirm the diagnosis</td><td>Once at diagnosis; again only if the diagnosis is in doubt</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Criteria met in all four clusters (intrusion, avoidance, negative mood and thinking, arousal); severity score 44, recorded as narrative</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Trauma-focused psychotherapy (cognitive processing therapy), sessions attended</td><td>Weekly for twelve sessions</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Cognitive processing therapy booked weekly from next week, with the interpreter</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depressive symptoms (PHQ-9)</td><td>Baseline, then monthly during active treatment</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 11 at intake, item 9 scored 0; next at six weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review, safety plan and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or self-harm; no firearm; 988 card carried</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Drinking since the last visit, against his own goal</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Three or four drinks most evenings, down from five or six</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Liver tests (AST, ALT, GGT) on naltrexone</td><td>Before starting, at four to six weeks, then every three to six months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Before naltrexone: AST 58, ALT 36, GGT 112 U/L at intake; AST under three times the upper limit; creatinine 1.0 mg/dL, eGFR 78</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>PEth, a blood marker of drinking over the past two to four weeks</td><td>At baseline and every one to three months during treatment, with consent</td><td>Up to date</td><td>236 ng/mL at intake; next at about eleven weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naltrexone safety: opioid use, nausea, wallet card</td><td>At the start and every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Urine test negative for opioids; wallet card given</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>SSRI safety: sodium, bleeding, falls and side effects</td><td>Sodium within the first month in older adults; review at each dose change</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No SSRI; he chose therapy first</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Falls and gait (Timed Up and Go), orthostatic blood pressure</td><td>At intake and every visit after a fall</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Timed Up and Go 13 seconds at intake; no fall since</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Traditional healing alongside clinic care, at his choice</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Offered again; his choice; nothing asked</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Psychiatry consultation</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Achebe-Varga today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Sleep most nights with nightmares no more than once a week. In progress; target 2026-10-28 [S]</item><item>Lower PTSD symptoms by a meaningful amount (PCL-5 down at least 10 to 20 points). In progress; target 2026-10-28 [S]</item><item>Drink no more than one drink on any day, and no drinking before riding. In progress; target 2026-10-28 [S]</item><item>No further falls. In progress; target 2026-10-28 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Management of Posttraumatic Stress Disorder and Acute Stress Disorder (2023); Clinical Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Posttraumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD) in Adults (2017); The American Psychiatric Association Practice Guideline for the Pharmacological Treatment of Patients With Alcohol Use Disorder (2018); The Healthcare Professional&apos;s Core Resource on Alcohol (2026); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); Special General Memorandum 94-08: Statement of Policy for the Traditional Cultural Advocacy Program (1994)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Six-week review with liver tests. Cognitive processing therapy weekly from next week. Call the clinic or 988 at any hour. Next scheduled visit 2026-08-19: six-week review: cognitive processing therapy, naltrexone, sleep, mood and medicine. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
